@@ -6,6 +6,8 @@ Convention adopted 2026-05-08 after the AEP-Plugin PR review surfaced confusion
 
 ## 2026-10-30
 
+- [x] **Task&I client speaks agent-exchange contract v2.1** (`devcontainer/taskandi-client.mjs`, `devcontainer/taskandi-usage.mjs`, `docs/taskandi-client.md`, `smoke/checks_28_taskandi_client.py`, `smoke/checks_29_taskandi_binding_usage.py`) — the client now targets `vibe-agent-exchange-v2.1`, the contract the Task&I session confirmed on 2026-10-30 (task_061). New: `next-task` (claims work, stores the claim in the outbox state), `complete` and `release` direct calls, `comment` and `record_time` jobs, richer `ask` (`order`, `estimate_minutes`, `remaining_minutes`, `reversible`, `blocking`, `in_reply_to`; result `{ask_id, placed}` stored on the job) and `list_asks` (owner-only actionable answers, `ignored` count). Payloads carry `task`: the binding task, or a `--task` that is the bound or claimed task (also on `usage`). A binding with no `contractRevision` is v2.1; pinning `vibe-action-window-fixture-v1` keeps v1 behaviour exactly, and v1 checks_28/29 only changed to pin it. Jobs queued under the old unpinned binding fail `flush` with `BINDING_CHANGED`; `status` lists them as `staleBindingKeys`. Fixtures only, no live Task&I claim.
+
 - [x] **Positioning follow-ups from the optimiser review** (`README.md`, `SECURITY.md`, `docs/codex-development.md`, `docs/codex-quickstart.md`, `site/src/content/home.md`) — corrected the new README claim that an interactive Codex `$vsss` doesn't survive exit (it hands off to the `codex-autonomy` background worker; the honest gap is fewer real overnight runs). SECURITY.md no longer says credentials "stay on the host": the per-repo PAT is readable inside the container, and keeping it on the host is now a proposal. The Codex development guide gains the "Claude recommended" pointer. README "Coming soon" no longer lists language profiles as unbuilt (`--profile python` shipped); auto-detect and more profiles remain.
 
 - [x] **Competitive check, and honest Claude-vs-Codex wording** (`docs/alternatives.md` (new), `README.md`, `ONBOARDING.md`, `docs/codex-quickstart.md`, `site/src/content/home.md`, `site/site-check.mjs`, `TODO.md`) — Martin asked whether vibe is still the best single-user way to run Claude Code autonomously. Web research (2026-10-30) says yes: nothing else combines local runs with LAN SSH, one token per repo, a default-deny firewall and a loop that keeps going across usage windows. Docker Sandboxes now isolates better (microVM, token kept on the host) and Conductor/Sculptor do parallel tasks better; three Martin-gated proposals are in TODO. `docs/alternatives.md` holds the table and sources; README gains "How vibe compares" and "Claude or Codex: which should lead?", saying plainly that vibe works better with Claude (native subagents for `/vs`, self-resuming `/vsss`, real slash commands, more live mileage). Onboarding, the Codex quickstart and the site's agent chip and body copy say the same. Also fixed a pre-existing site-check failure: the `/ask` chip named `gpt-6-astra`, which `ask.md` stopped hard-coding when model ids became "newest of each class".
